{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if in My statutes, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says ״וְאִם״ and did not rely on the ״וְאִם״ said at the beginning of the passage? Further, it said <b>in My statutes</b> — but it should have said ״וְאִם חֻקֹּתַי תִּמְאָסוּ״, for their annulment would not stand [as sufficient] without [also rejecting] the judgments, as in what it says adjacent to it, ״וְאִם אֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי״. Further, why did it return a second time to say ״וְאִם״? Further, why did the verse not combine statutes and judgments together and say ״וְאִם חֻקֹּתַי וּמִשְׁפָּטַי תִּמְאַס אוֹ תִּגְעַל״, etc.? Further, why did the verse single out rejection (מְאִיסָה) for statutes and loathing of soul (גְעִילַת נֶפֶשׁ) for judgments? Further, after it mentioned rejection and loathing, what need is there to say ״לְבִלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת״ — and all the more so since above it wrote ״וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ אֵת כָּל הַמִּצְוֹת״? And although we explained in one of the approaches that it is a continuation of what preceded it, with all this, the meaning of the verse still requires resolution. Further, what is the intent of His saying ״לְהַפְרְכֶם״, etc.?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is against the seduction of the evil inclination, which entices a person to nullify negative commandments, and it schemes to begin by distancing a person's mind from commandments that are חֻקָּה without reason, in one of two ways: either it says to him that the things said by the Elohim of the universe are not to be believed, or it says to him — granted we believe He said them — the matter is not according to its plain sense, and He does not intend what is heard from them. And when a man inclines his ear to the words of the seducer, then it adds to speak to him also regarding commandments that do have reason, in the commandments of HaShem, and violating the word of HaShem in any respect will become easy for him because of the flawed reasons it shows him, since he has distanced himself from holiness. And regarding a transgression for which the inclination finds no reason, it returns to argue from those transgressions to which this person has already become rooted in committing, and says to him: ״Tell me, what evil and punishment has come upon you for having transgressed several transgressions that the Torah forbade?״ And it does not remind him of the cause of the beginning of his transgressing them, which was through the argument that the inclination's instructions made plausible to him, and it strengthens itself against him with the aid of desire that overpowers him, and he transgresses the word of HaShem. And after he has transgressed the word of HaShem once or twice, then it comes with yet another argument suited to its purpose — that the punishment will be lighter for him if he becomes a mummar (apostate) and denies everything, for then He will not punish him more severely if he reaches the point of punishment than for one sin — that he exchanged and broke the covenant he swore at Mount Sinai — whereas otherwise he would be punished for each and every detail." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the degrees of the seducer, the verse comes and speaks of each one, the first first: it began to entice through commandments that are חֻקָּה — <b>and if in My statutes</b> — which are commandments that have no reason, as the Sages said (Numbers Rabbah 19) that the Satan and the nations of the world say: ״What is this commandment and what reason is there in it?״ Therefore it was precise to say <b>in My statutes</b> — with a bet of causation — to say that it is because they are statutes that you rejected them and accepted the words of the inclination. And it says ״וְאִם״ to say that this is a different degree, which is the nullification even of negative commandments, and also to say that each one individually is sufficient to bring about the punishment stated in the passage." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the second seduction of the inclination it says <b>and if et My judgments</b> — which are commandments that do have reason — <b>your soul shall loathe</b>. And the reason it changed from what is said regarding statutes, according to our approach, has a correct explanation: for statutes, the absence of reason is what caused the rejection (מְאִיסָה) of them, whereas judgments, which are straight of reason — their cause is that the soul coveted doing evil, not that there is no reason in the commandment. Therefore it says ״תִּגְעַל נַפְשְׁכֶם״, and therefore it did not also say ״בְּמִשְׁפָּטַי״ as it said ״בְּחֻקֹּתַי״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>תִּגְעַל</b>, according to the statement of the pious ones regarding a person's examination of his soul to know what it is — whether it stands in the category of good or is an impure soul — for the matter is recognized according to the soul's desire toward which it inclines to act; see his words in Sefer HaYashar by R. Tam (Gate 9). It is the statement ״תִּגְעַל נַפְשְׁכֶם״, for the wicked soul loathes the fulfillment of commandments and chooses to do evil because of the sicknesses within it, which are of the aspect of evil, as the Sages said (Berakhot 61a): the wicked — their evil inclination rules them. And this is what Scripture concludes: <b>so as not to perform all My commandments</b>, for this soul loathes all branches of holiness. And His saying <b>to break [your covenant]</b> corresponds to the final seduction of the evil inclination, which says to him: break the covenant and exchange your honor, to lighten the punishment; and the verse comes and forewarns complete punishment in equal measure also for this, and nullifies the argument of the seducer in this particular detail, as the Prophet says (Ezekiel 25:8) ״הִנֵּה כְּכָל הַגּוֹיִם בֵּית יְהוּדָה״, etc., and the response to this was (Ezekiel 20:33) ״אִם לֹא בְּחֵמָה שְׁפוּכָה אֶמְלוֹךְ עֲלֵיכֶם״." "<b>Further,</b> the verses may be explained according to the words of the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Zohar 1:100a), that the perfection of divine service lies in the balance of three things: thought, speech, and action. Therefore, when HaShem repeated the condition, He mentioned all three: corresponding to speech He said <b>וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ</b> — corresponding to the engagement of Torah, which is the verbal reasoning; corresponding to action He said <b>וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ</b> etc.; and corresponding to thought He said <b>וְאִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי</b> ״תִּמְאָסוּ״, and this matter pertains to thought. And since thought resides in both the brain and the heart, therefore, corresponding to the brain He said ״תִּמְאָסוּ״, and corresponding to the heart He said ״תִּגְעַל נַפְשְׁכֶם״, for the soul is in the heart, as is known." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that the verse speaks corresponding to the gradations that exist within the totality of the people of Israel, and they are three classes: the first are the great ones of Israel who stand by the Torah and the divine service, and corresponding to this class He said <b>וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ לִי</b> etc., for the class of the righteous need to be precise in the commandments of HaShem and to fulfill the entire Torah, and if they are negligent even in a minor branch, they are held accountable with their lives, as our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, stated (Yoma 86b), and this is their language: What constitutes a profanation of HaShem's name? For example, I who walk four cubits without tzitzit and without tefillin and without Torah. And concerning them the verse said <b>וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ לִי</b>, meaning, to exert oneself in the engagement of Torah for its own sake, <b>וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ אֵת כָּל הַמִּצְווֹת</b> in their general principles and their specific details and their minutiae, for even a slight deficiency is accounted as great intentional sin. And there is another class who are not men of Torah but who are intelligent and know the recognition of good and right; concerning them He said <b>וְאִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תִּמְאָסוּ</b>, for these the verse will punish even for the neglect of commandments that have no stated reason, and it will be accounted to them as intentional sins; but as for the neglect of the minutiae for which the great ones of Israel are punished, the same punishment will not reach them. And there is yet another class, who are the simpletons among the people of Israel, whose knowledge does not extend to demonstrating the necessity of fulfilling the statutes in the Torah, and concerning them He said <b>וְאִם אֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי תִּגְעַל נַפְשְׁכֶם</b>, for these will not be punished with the great punishment stated in the matter except for the neglect of the mishpatim, which are comprehensible to human reason; but for the hukkim, and certainly the minutiae for which the great ones of Israel are punished, they are judged concerning them as unintentional sinners and as coerced fools whose punishment is very light indeed." "<b>לִבְלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת.</b> Here HaShem recorded a condition: that He will not bring all the stated punishment unless they are still wholeheartedly agreed in their minds to walk in the ways of evil — ״לִבְלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת״ etc.; but if their intent is to return, or even if their intent is not to return but the Master, blessed be He, knows that they will return in repentance, HaShem will lengthen His patience toward them until they return to Him, and He will not bring upon them the written evil." "<b>לְהַפְרְכֶם אֶת בְּרִיתִי.</b> This intends yet another condition: that He will not bring all the punishment except upon one who transgresses to provoke anger, as it says ״לְהַפְרְכֶם אֶת בְּרִיתִי״; but one who transgresses out of desire will not enter the category of punishments. And our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing, said (Torat Kohanim): ״וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״ etc. — there is a person who does not study but does perform; therefore it says ״וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״. There is a person who neither studies nor performs but does not despise others; therefore it says ״בְּחֻקֹּתַי תִּמְאָסוּ״ etc. — one who does not study, does not perform, and despises others who do perform, and hates the Sages — as it says ״תִּגְעַל נַפְשְׁכֶם״; and ultimately he prevents others from performing — as it says ״לְבִלְתִּי עֲשׂוֹת״; and ultimately he denies the fundamental principle — as it says ״לְהַפְרְכֶם״ etc., end quote. Now according to their words, may their memory be for a blessing, the stated punishment will not come unless they reach the final boundary, which is denying the fundamental principle. And the reason the verse did not state only the final boundary is to teach the cause of causes that will surround a person to bring him to this last stage, and from the implication of the words you will understand that when he stumbles in the first stage, he will roll and descend lower and lower until the last stage. And from this we may now understand the statement of the prophet (Jeremiah 9:12) ״עַל מָה אָבְדָה הָאָרֶץ עַל עָזְבָם אֶת תּוֹרָתִי״ — which is the first cause from which all the evil particulars emerged, may the Merciful One save us."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {4 "The citation from Ezekiel 25:8 appears to follow a midrashic application rather than the plain sense of that chapter's verse; the reference is rendered as found in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:38.000260358Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}